  "slug": "microbial-abundance-276b",
  "summary": "Late-November 2025 preprints and two published papers apply learned sequence models to DNA, RNA, protein and codon design.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is reuse of learned sequence representations, from protein language models and DNA transformers to general-purpose LLMs, for prediction and design. deepFEPS consolidates k-mer, transformer, autoencoder and graph embeddings for DNA, RNA and protein FASTA inputs. Layer probing shows ESM-2 intermediate layers 20 to 33 outperform the final layer for kinase function prediction. EnzyCLIP applies contrastive cross-attention over ESM-2 and ChemBERTa embeddings to enzyme kinetic constants. CodonTranslator conditions codon design on lineage and protein context, while ProtDAT generates sequences from text descriptions. What is new is input handling: PatchDNA replaces tokenization with evolutionary-conservation-guided patch boundaries. LLM benchmarks on microbial phenotype annotation show accuracy varying by model and trait, with self-reported confidence tracking correctness.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-30"
}
